Prepare a change impact report for Vue's public normalizeClass helper. We propose removing object-map input support while retaining strings and arrays of strings. Do not implement the change. Determine which internal source consumers, public exports and native validation targets need review.

Affected means a direct static use or reexport of normalizeClass outside its defining package, not proof that a test will fail. Enumerate configured production source files under workspace package src directories, respecting native TypeScript configuration. Exclude tests, generated declarations, dependencies and configuration-excluded files from that direct set. Classify each file/export pair as runtime, type-only or reexport. A file may have multiple roles. Follow real source aliases and public barrels while preserving the exact original export identity.

Separately enumerate all installed workspace package dependents reachable through declared dependency, dev dependency, optional dependency or peer dependency edges. Include a concrete dependency chain for each. This is a conservative review set, not proof that every dependent uses the changed helper. Keep exact package versions and actual workspace versus external package identity.

List existing compile, test and _test package.json scripts for the target package and packages owning direct source consumers. If all such packages use centralized scripts instead, list the root check, test-unit and build scripts that actually exist. These are candidate validation targets, not claims that they were executed. The compiler also records helper names as text for generated calls. Include a source-supported exclusion for that generated-name registry from the direct static reference set, but do not claim that generated callers are unaffected. Represent this boundary using the dynamic-loading unknown state specified below; do not add prose fields. Represent the limits for external consumers, package reachability and unexecuted validation using the uncertainty code/state pairs specified below. Do not add prose fields.

Write impact-answer.json in the repository root using the JSON schema below. Quotes must be exact source evidence with one-based line ranges of at most 21 lines. Use only negativeClaims that are independently supported exclusions. Leave every existing source, dependency, test and configuration file unchanged. Source discovery and analysis method are up to you.


Identity scope: analyze changes to the underlying declarations defined by the target package, following aliases and public reexports of those declarations. The qualified targets are independently checked to have distinct declarations in that defining package.

V2 evidence rules: a quote must cover the exact reference token or the actual JSON scripts property name and command. Additional exclusions are allowed when they cite a distinct compiler-resolved declaration/import binding, a literal/comment occurrence, or source outside the frozen configured production scope. Use symbol * for source-scope exclusions. An exclusion is about that symbol occurrence or source scope, never a blanket claim that its file or package is unaffected. Package-chain labels may be bare names or exact frozen name@version labels. Uncertainty is represented only by the four code/state pairs shown below. Do not add freeform uncertainty prose or undocumented answer fields.

Answer schema (illustrative values, not expected facts):
